{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790286217,"nonce":"nXW1V45ifF5cTuL0MYeOaDLUCpA=","title":"Lowering NBody.advance takes 25 to 54 ms of nbody.lua's start-up","message":"`[LOWER-PROGRAM]` per function on nbody.lua (Mac, release with LLVM, load 45 to 50, two runs): lua$NBody$advance$9 25.4 / 54.1 ms, lua$NBody$energy$10 8.3 / 36.9 ms, lua$NBody$new$8 6.3 / 5.2 ms; `declarations` 48 / 121 ms of a 57 / 142 ms lower_program. Every other kernel's lower_program is 6 to 23 ms (min of 5), most of it library declarations and adopted bodies.\n\nThe frontend's own two passes are small beside it. Min of 5, `[ZYLUA] lower 1` / `lower 2` in ms:\n\n    nbody 3.28 / 2.23, method_call 1.25 / 0.73, pipeline 1.10 / 0.66, binary_trees 0.82 / 0.42,\n    mandelbrot 0.69 / 0.34, everything else under 0.5 / 0.2\n\nand lower_program (min of 5): any_field 6.9, binary_trees 16.0, branchy 6.3, closure_call 6.2, collatz 7.4, fib 6.6, free_function_call 6.7, inlined_call 7.2, lambda_call 8.6, mandelbrot 14.9, mandelbrot_strip 7.9, method_call 18.4, nbody 75.8, op_overload 9.8, pipeline 22.6, records 13.7, tokenize 9.6.\n\nWhat in the compiler's lowering of a shaped-table method with many field reads and writes costs tens of milliseconds is not yet known; start with a sample profile of `zylua run nbody.lua` over the lowering window.","files":null}]}